  "question": {
    "text": "For the hyperbola $\\frac{x^2}{\\cos ^2 \\alpha}-\\frac{y^2}{\\sin ^2 \\alpha}=1$, which of the following remains constant when $\\alpha$ varies?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "PIaPb",
      "text": "\neccentricity\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "rPq0d",
      "text": "\ndirectrix\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bAVnl",
      "text": "\nabscissae of vertices\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "OqJtB",
      "text": "\nabscissae of foci",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$a^2=\\cos ^2 \\alpha$ and $b^2=\\sin ^2 \\alpha$\nCoordinates of focii are $(\\pm a e, 0)$\n$\\therefore b^2=a^2\\left(e^2-1\\right) \\Rightarrow e=\\sec \\alpha$.\nHence abscissae of foci remain constant when $\\alpha$ varies.",
    "image": null
  },
